Synthesis
---------
LRC: n<=13 are the genuinely easier/proved values.  Among later values,
n=15,16,18,19,22,24 have cleaner local C=2n-1 carriers than n=14 in
this scorecard; n=19 is the prettiest because n and C=37 are both prime.
The caution is that this is local-carrier ease, not a proof that LRC(19)
is globally easier than finishing the immediate n=14 frontier.

Unit distance: for lower-bound or unit-spine certificates, n=13 and n=14
are easier than n=21 because they are the one-slab THM-408 rows.  The
centered Eisenstein shell n=19 is a promising symmetric side target.
For exact upper-bound work, n=22 is not easier than n=21 despite having
an explicit P_2^+ spine, because the repo's n=22 obstacle is precisely
the extra endpoint/ear side channel.

